From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 05:15:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.security.authentication.onlineid: implement
 WebProviderError, all three properties.

Excel and PowerPoint both fault on a null read, every run, on the WAM path:

    wine: Unhandled page fault on read access to 0000000000000000
      at address ...  (mso30win32client.dll +0x5775f4)

The containing function came from .pdata, not the PDB -- measured on this
binary, only 3.1% of Mso30Win32Client.pdb's 59,585 .text publics land on a
.pdata function start and the delta histogram has no mode, so its addresses
are unusable even though its GUID/age match the PE (see the docs). The
function stages a {line, tag, file, function} context on the stack three
instructions before it needs it, and reading what those two pointers point at
names the caller outright:

    "...\winrt\Windows.Security.Authentication.Web.Core.h"
    "auto __cdecl winrt::impl::consume_Windows_Security_Authentication_Web_
     Core_IWebProviderError<...>"

C++/WinRT's generated property getter is

    WebProviderError ResponseError() const
    {
        void* value{};
        check_hresult(...->get_ResponseError(&value));
        return WebProviderError{ value, take_ownership_from_abi };
    }

-- it checks the HRESULT and then wraps whatever pointer came back without
testing it. So the old `*error = NULL; return S_OK;` was a promise the caller
took literally, and its next line was a virtual call through nothing. Same
shape as the E_NOINTERFACE-on-a-map gap already recorded in webtoken.c.

The IID is not in this prefix's metadata (Windows.Security.winmd is absent),
so it came from the name/guid table C++/WinRT leaves in WebView2Host.dll -- a
UTF-16 interface name followed by its IID at the next 16-byte boundary. The
rule was checked against IWebTokenRequestResult, whose IID this file already
carried, before its answer for IWebProviderError was used.

**Implementing two of the three properties was not enough, and the way it
failed is the point.** With ErrorCode and ErrorMessage only, the null read
was gone and replaced by an access violation at Office's CFG dispatch thunk
(`jmp rax`) with a garbage target. A stack scan at the fault (the kernelbase
diagnostic in the companion patch) gave the caller, whose own staged context
read `consume_..._IWebProviderError<...>::Properties(void) const`, and whose
call site is `mov rax,[rax+0x40]` -- vtable slot 8, one past the end of an
eight-entry vtable. A WinRT interface implemented with most of its methods is
not partially working; it is a live jump into whatever .rdata follows the
table. Count the methods against the metadata, not against what the caller
has been seen to use.

With all three, Excel reaches its Start screen and creates a workbook.

Also adds TRACEs to the provider getters, which had none, and records in a
comment the FindAccountProviderAsync change that was tried and reverted: two
byte-for-byte different answers to that call produced one identical fault, so
whatever remains there is not on this side of the boundary.
